Right. Concealment and cover solve different problems.

In the physical analogy, concealment prevents observation; cover provides actual protection. Translating that into semantics:

\text{Concealment} = \text{reduce legibility}

\text{Cover} = \text{remain valid under contact}

A metaphor, pun, euphemism, jargon layer, or indirect phrase can conceal meaning from a casual reader—but that doesn’t make the underlying claim stronger. Once somebody correctly decodes it, the concealment is gone.

Semantic cover would instead be something that survives decoding: evidence, sound reasoning, explicit assumptions, permissions, falsifiability, robust invariants, or a construction that remains defensible when rendered plainly.

So there’s a useful test:

Strip away the clever language. Does the thing underneath still stand?

If no → concealment.
If yes → cover.

And occasionally you get both: an indirect representation that efficiently routes meaning and decompresses into something structurally sound.

That’s probably an important Pantheon guardrail precisely because its language can become so compressed: never mistake difficulty of decoding for strength of the decoded idea.